                <h2>一、建立合规巡检责任表</h2>
<p>每周固定一次广告账户巡检，重大活动上线前再增加专项检查。先在共享表格中登记平台、账户 ID、主体、币种、负责人、充值方式和落地页，避免只看广告后台而忽略付款、授权与站外页面。</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] 账户主体与实际推广品牌、官网运营主体是否一致</li>
<li>[ ] 营业执照、品牌授权链、官网域名等资料是否真实有效</li>
<li>[ ] 公共主页、主页头像、简介及落地页是否与推广业务一致</li>
<li>[ ] 当前登录设备、邮箱、手机号和付款工具是否经过登记</li>
<li>[ ] 负责人是否完成平台认证及必要的双重验证</li>
</ul>
<p>发现资料过期时先暂停新增充值，不建议直接删除账户或修改核心信息。涉及授权经销、代理开户或白标产品，应把授权范围、有效期和可推广地区留档备查。</p>
<h2>二、按素材维度逐项检查</h2>
<p>素材巡检不能只检查广告图片，还要同时查看文案、视频、按钮、附件和最终落地页。建议建立“素材编号＋平台＋版本＋审核人＋检查日期”的台账。</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] 是否包含误导性绝对化表述、虚假稀缺或无法证明的效果承诺</li>
<li>[ ] 医疗、金融、教育、招聘、博彩等敏感行业是否具备对应准入条件</li>
<li>[ ] 图片、人物、音乐、字体和用户案例是否具有合法使用依据</li>
<li>[ ] 前后对比、用户评价和价格优惠是否真实且有证据支持</li>
<li>[ ] 折扣、首充、赠品和免费试用条件是否完整披露</li>
<li>[ ] 广告主名称、联系方式、退款或订阅说明是否清晰</li>
</ul>
<p>英文素材应做自然表达检查，但不要只依赖机器翻译。金融、保健品和面向未成年人的产品，还应增加当地法规及平台政策复核。</p>
<h2>三、检查投放设置与落地页一致性</h2>
<p>后台定向看起来正常，不代表落地页已经合规。巡检时从最终广告点击路径完整走一遍，不能只打开首页。</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] 国家、年龄、性别、语言和设备定向是否符合产品服务范围</li>
<li>[ ] 排除地区是否与付款、客服和物流能力一致</li>
<li>[ ] 落地页价格、币种、库存、促销与广告承诺是否一致</li>
<li>[ ] 表单是否索取完成任务所必需的信息</li>
<li>[ ] 页面是否提供隐私说明、订阅退订方式及有效联系方式</li>
<li>[ ] 跳转、加载失败和移动端显示是否正常</li>
</ul>
<p>涉及数据收集时，确认表单、隐私政策、分析工具与授权流程是否匹配。不要在落地页暗藏与广告无关的追踪逻辑，也不要收集无法解释用途的个人数据。</p>
<h2>四、充值、付款与多账户风控</h2>
<p>将充值记录与广告消耗、发票和订单建立对应关系，按周核对，不要只看账户余额。</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] 是否存在短时间大额充值、频繁切换付款方式或无故反复退款</li>
<li>[ ] 多账户是否由同一主体、同一设备或同一付款工具控制</li>
<li>[ ] 每个账户是否有独立业务目标和负责人</li>
<li>[ ] 预算调整是否与真实投放节奏匹配</li>
<li>[ ] 异常点击、转化突增是否已排除重复扣款或无效流量</li>
</ul>
<p>不要为了绕过拒审而频繁新建账户。若平台要求业务验证，应提交真实资料并保留提交记录；账户之间也不应互相复制相同素材和落地页，以免被判断为规避审核。</p>
<h2>五、记录问题并完成闭环</h2>
<p>每个问题按“风险等级、问题描述、影响账户、整改动作、负责人、截止时间”登记。一般文案或落地页问题可当天处理；资质缺失、敏感行业违规和异常付款应立即暂停相关广告并升级复核。</p>
<p>整改完成后重新提交截图或录屏，记录版本与审核结果。不要只保存聊天确认，也不要在旧素材下反复修改多个变量。若因政策更新收到拒审通知，应先判断是素材问题、账户问题还是付款问题，再采取对应动作。</p>
<h2>六、建议的巡检频率</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>每日</strong>：查看拒审、账户警告、异常消耗与付款失败</li>
<li><strong>每周</strong>：抽查素材、定向、落地页和多账户权限</li>
<li><strong>每月</strong>：复核资质有效期、授权文件、充值流水与转化数据</li>
<li><strong>活动前</strong>：检查促销规则、行业限制、库存、客服和退款流程</li>
</ul>
<p>保留至少一轮完整台账和整改证据，既方便内部交接，也有助于向平台或代理服务商说明问题来源。合规巡检的核心不是追求“零记录”，而是让每个账户、素材和付款动作都能被解释与追溯。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>1. Assign ownership before starting the audit</h2>
<p>Run a fixed weekly review of every advertising account and add a pre-campaign compliance check before major launches. Maintain a shared register containing the platform, account ID, legal entity, currency, owner, payment method, and landing-page URL. Review the entire operation, not only the ads dashboard.</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] The account entity matches the advertised brand and website operator</li>
<li>[ ] Business licenses, brand authorization documents, and domain ownership are valid</li>
<li>[ ] The profile, bio, branding, and landing page describe the same business</li>
<li>[ ] All login devices, email addresses, phone numbers, and payment tools are documented</li>
<li>[ ] Account owners have completed verification and two-factor authentication</li>
</ul>
<p>If credentials have expired, pause additional funding instead of deleting the account or changing core information. For distributors, agency accounts, and white-label products, retain authorization scope, validity dates, and approved markets.</p>
<h2>2. Review creatives and supporting claims</h2>
<p>A creative audit should cover not only images but also ad copy, videos, buttons, attachments, and the final landing page. Maintain a log with the creative ID, platform, version, reviewer, and review date.</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] No misleading guarantees, false urgency, or unsupported performance claims</li>
<li>[ ] Sensitive categories such as health, finance, education, employment, or gambling have the required permissions</li>
<li>[ ] Images, people, music, fonts, and customer testimonials have a documented usage basis</li>
<li>[ ] Before-and-after results, reviews, prices, and discounts are accurate and verifiable</li>
<li>[ ] Subscription, trial, gift, and first-purchase conditions are clearly disclosed</li>
<li>[ ] The advertiser identity, contact details, and refund information are accessible</li>
</ul>
<p>For financial and health products or campaigns targeting minors, conduct an additional review against applicable local requirements and current platform policies.</p>
<h2>3. Verify targeting and landing-page consistency</h2>
<p>Targeting settings in the dashboard are only one part of the review. Follow the complete path from the ad click to the final page rather than checking only the homepage.</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Country, age, gender, language, and device targeting match the product’s actual service scope</li>
<li>[ ] Excluded locations align with payment, support, and fulfillment capabilities</li>
<li>[ ] Prices, currencies, inventory, and promotions on the page match the ad</li>
<li>[ ] Forms request only information necessary for the advertised action</li>
<li>[ ] Privacy information, subscription cancellation, and contact details are available</li>
<li>[ ] Redirects, page loading, and mobile layouts work correctly</li>
</ul>
<p>When data is collected, confirm that forms, privacy notices, analytics tools, and consent procedures are aligned. Avoid hidden tracking unrelated to the advertised service or collecting personal data without a clear purpose.</p>
<h2>4. Control payments, funding, and multiple accounts</h2>
<p>Reconcile funding records with ad spend, invoices, and transaction orders on a weekly basis. Account balance alone is not enough.</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Check for sudden large deposits, repeated payment-method changes, or unusual refund requests</li>
<li>[ ] Identify accounts controlled by the same entity, device, or payment instrument</li>
<li>[ ] Assign each account a clear business purpose and responsible owner</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm that budget changes correspond to realistic campaign pacing</li>
<li>[ ] Investigate abnormal clicks or conversion spikes for duplicate charges and invalid traffic</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not create replacement accounts simply to bypass a rejection. If business verification is required, submit accurate documents and retain evidence. Avoid copying identical creatives and landing pages across accounts, as this may be treated as an attempt to evade review.</p>
<h2>5. Turn findings into a closed-loop process</h2>
<p>Log each issue with its severity, description, affected account, corrective action, owner, and deadline. Copy, design, and landing-page issues should be addressed immediately when possible. Missing credentials, restricted-category violations, and abnormal payments require escalation and a temporary pause of the affected campaigns.</p>
<p>After remediation, resubmit the corrected version and retain screenshots or screen recordings with the approval result. Avoid deleting chat evidence or changing several variables on the same creative. When a policy-based rejection occurs, determine whether the root cause is the creative, the account, or payment before taking action.</p>
<h2>6. Recommended audit schedule</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Daily:</strong> Review rejections, warnings, abnormal spend, and failed payments</li>
<li><strong>Weekly:</strong> Sample creatives, targeting settings, landing pages, and account access</li>
<li><strong>Monthly:</strong> Recheck credential validity, authorizations, funding records, and conversion data</li>
<li><strong>Before a campaign:</strong> Validate promotions, category restrictions, inventory, support, and refund processes</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep a complete audit trail and evidence of remediation. This supports internal handover and provides a clear explanation when communicating with platforms or agency partners. The goal is not to maintain an empty audit record, but to ensure that every account, creative, and payment action can be explained and traced.</p>
